  <section>
    <h2>About Dexter</h2>
    <p>Dexter's historical identity is shaped by the Dexter woolen mill complex, which operated from the 1840s until 1977. The mill site on the Sebasticook River is one of Maine's most architecturally significant industrial ruins — a brick-and-granite complex that has been the subject of multiple preservation studies. While the mill itself is closed, the surrounding mill-worker housing and the town's downtown layout still reflect the mid-19th-century mill-town pattern.</p>
    <p>Today, Dexter's economy combines small-business retail, light manufacturing, agriculture, and a substantial commuter population that drives to Bangor for work. The town's lakes — Lake Wassookeag, Puffers Pond, and the Sebasticook River — provide recreational opportunities for both residents and summer visitors. Population has been modestly declining (Dexter lost roughly 2-3% of its population between 2010 and 2020), typical of small rural Maine mill towns.</p>
    <p>For the cannabis market, Dexter is significant as the southern anchor of the Piscataquis County interior dispensary cluster. Puffers Place's on-site lodging property makes it a destination rather than a transit stop, attracting visitors who might otherwise skip the town entirely.</p>
  </section>

